Fair challenge, and he's partly right. I said the photos fade and I'd resent being lectured, then gave F the most points anyway, which doesn't add up. I look at numbers all the time with yellow stickers and own-brand prices, so a calorie badge fits how I already shop. The photos would make me wince for a couple of days and then I'd tune them out.

But he's also right that being skint cuts the other way. If I'm buying a snack at all, it's usually the cheap, filling one, and 240 versus 95 might just tell me the Dairy Milk is better value per bite. So B won't do as much as I'd like. I'd still put it first, with F behind it for the short-term shock, and keep A, C and D low.

{"points": {"A": 10, "B": 48, "C": 5, "D": 2, "E": 0, "F": 35}, "most_ignored": "E", "why": "Calorie numbers fit how I already compare prices so B does most of the work, while photos shock briefly and plain warnings or no labels would just wash over me."}

Fair points, mostly. I did say graphic photos would feel preachy and I'd get used to them, then gave them the top score anyway. In the moment at the machine they'd make me wince, but within a week I'd be looking past them, and they'd annoy me like the campaigns I can't stand. The calorie badge is the same story. 95 versus 240 made me shrug, and I buy on price and tiredness, not a number on a wrapper.

Still, I don't think plain text is as powerful as my friend suggests. I'd probably skim it, though it's the tone I trust and it doesn't feel like blame. Calories plus a sober line is a decent combination, and it fits how I changed with beef: quiet, repeated, and then normal. So I'm shifting points from C and D to A and B, keeping C a bit ahead because the shock does work on day one. F stays near zero.

{"points": {"A": 24, "B": 24, "C": 26, "D": 9, "E": 13, "F": 4}, "most_ignored": "F", "why": "Unlabelled snacks give me nothing new, and I'd get used to the shock photos quickly, so the quieter calorie-plus-text combination is more realistic for me."}

Fair points, some of them. F getting 12 was me dodging: a label-free vending machine changes nothing, so that should be near zero. And E at 20 was daft given I said I'd resent it. Graphic photos would put me off for a week, then I'd tune them out and get irritated.

Where he's wrong is the calorie badge. I don't scrimp on food, but a number on the Gü dessert or the millionaire shortbread is something I'd actually see, and it works as a comparison against the fruit pot at 95. It's the only one that gives me something to act on. But he's right about the cholesterol. The GP's comment worked on me because it was sober and official, so a plain "heart disease" line in A does land a bit more than I admitted. D on its own is the same tone but gives me no number, so it stays low, though not nothing.

{"points": {"A": 27, "B": 6, "C": 34, "D": 9, "E": 21, "F": 3}, "most_ignored": "F", "why": "A label-free machine changes nothing, and the graphic photos mostly just irritate me, while a calorie number with a sober NHS-style line is what I'd actually act on."}

Fair enough, the mate has a point on a few things. The badge is doing the work, and I was a bit daft giving F 26 while saying I'd resent it. If the photo's on there it's because the badge is there too, and I'd be noticing the number, not the tumour. E being near F doesn't hold up either. It's the same number with a lecture bolted on, so it should sit below B.

But I'll stand by B being the one that works. The blood test showed me a number from the doctor and I acted on it, so a plain figure on the pack lands with me in a way a telling-off doesn't. He's right that I'd probably stop seeing it by Thursday, and that I'm not often at a vending machine anyway, so the overall effect is modest. The photos get a bit of credit for a wince, but not much.

{"points": {"A": 6, "B": 56, "C": 2, "D": 0, "E": 14, "F": 22}, "most_ignored": "D", "why": "A plain calorie number is a fact I can weigh up against my money without being lectured, while the text warning is wallpaper and the photos only work for a wince before I resent them."}

Fair points, some of them. I do buy the same list every Thursday, and a number on a Dairy Milk isn't going to move a shopping habit that's driven by price. The badge helps a bit when I'm standing in front of a machine or deciding on a long-run snack, but I was probably flattering myself on how much it changes.

I'll give the friend the blood pressure point. What got me to cut back was a blunt fact about my own body, so the plain text about heart disease isn't pure noise to me. It still reads like a telling-off, though, and I'd stop seeing it after a few weeks. The photos I'd flinch at, but I don't think they'd change what I buy. I'd just resent them. So I'll nudge F up a little and bring E down a bit, and I'm not shifting much on B and C.

{"points": {"A": 5, "B": 12, "C": 18, "D": 0, "E": 35, "F": 30}, "most_ignored": "D", "why": "The calorie badge still gives me something usable without a lecture, but my blood pressure scare shows blunt health facts do land, so the text-plus-badge combo deserves more than I first gave it."}

Fair points, some of them. I did say the photos would nudge me most and then put the plain facts on top, and that's me wanting to be the sensible fact-loving fella. But I still don't think the shock photo would keep working. I'd see the bleeding eye the first few times, and by week three I'd be walking past it like the posters at the surgery. The calorie number is different, because I can use it without anyone shouting at me.

Still, he's right about the biscuits. I'm not going to put the Dairy Milk back because it says 240. And the tea sugar only changed when the nurse put my own numbers in front of me. A diabetic eye picture is the closest thing here to that, since I've got the condition. It would hit me harder than a plain line of text, so A should be well ahead of B. A with calories still beats D because the number gives me something to compare. I'll shift some points from C towards A, and I'll cut B a bit, since I'd read that once and ignore it.

{"points": {"A": 36, "B": 12, "C": 28, "D": 12, "E": 6, "F": 6}, "most_ignored": "F", "why": "Unlabelled snacks tell me nothing, and the plain text warning I'd read once and forget, while the diabetic eye photo with a calorie number is the closest thing to the nurse showing me my own numbers."}

Fair points, some of them. The friend is right that "in the moment" is when I'd actually choose, so I've probably undersold F. A photo of a bowel tumour on a Dairy Milk would make me hesitate for a second, even if I'd resent it. And yes, I'm not disciplined, so "usable information" is partly me flattering myself. But the number is still the only thing I could tie to my running, and that's what changed my breakfast, so I'd keep E on top. I'd also admit C is basically B with a sentence I'd tune out, so they sit together at the bottom.

I'd shift some points from E to F and D, trim the gap, and make C and B nearly equal.

{"points": {"A": 4, "B": 1, "C": 1, "D": 20, "E": 42, "F": 32}, "most_ignored": "B", "why": "Calorie badges still give me something I can use for running, the gory photos would bite a bit more in the moment than I admitted, and a plain-text warning is basically the same as no label at all."}

Fair points, to be honest. I did give myself too much credit on the calorie sums. At the end of the month I'm buying whatever's cheapest, and a number on a fruit pot won't make it cost less. The photos are exactly the tone I can't stand, so E at 20 doesn't fit with what I said. I'd wince, then stop seeing it.

The plain warning does sound more like the NHS site than the Facebook stuff, so I'll give it more credit. I still think the calorie badge is the only thing that gives me something to use, even if I use it less than I claimed. Mostly nothing changes what ends up in the trolley, so the points are low and spread thin.

{"points": {"A": 4, "B": 12, "C": 33, "D": 0, "E": 12, "F": 39}, "most_ignored": "D", "why": "Calories plus a sober NHS-style warning is the only mix I'd trust, but my budget and my biscuit habit mean none of it changes much, and the gory photos just feel like being blamed."}

My friend's got a point on a few things. I do hunt yellow stickers for the price, not the number, and I've never counted calories in my life. A bare "240" doesn't mean much without knowing what a day's worth is, so I'd probably glance and shrug. And the dentist thing is fair: being told bluntly and personally is what actually worked on me, so I can't pretend I'm immune to a shock.

I'd still say the plain text warning is wallpaper, and I'd still bristle at the photos. But I'd probably look away from them and still feel put off the chocolate in the moment, which is more than a number would do. A calorie badge does help a bit when it's a comparison, like 95 next to 240, but I overstated it. So I'd shift some points toward the graphic ones, trim the badge-only option, and keep the plain text low. D stays the most ignored, since nothing changes there, though C is a close second.

{"points": {"A": 30, "B": 25, "C": 4, "D": 0, "E": 20, "F": 21}, "most_ignored": "D", "why": "Unlabelled snacks give me nothing at all, and the plain text warning would be wallpaper, while the graphic photos would actually stick in my head even if they annoy me."}

Fair points, some of them. I do buy from a Sunday shop and eat what's in the house, so a calorie badge I'd compare in a vending machine matters less in my real week. And yes, F at 30 next to C at 35 was inconsistent: if text is wallpaper, it shouldn't be earning me points. The dentist thing is true too. What worked was someone making it personal and concrete, so the photos probably stick longer than I wanted to admit, even though I'd resent them.

I still think the calorie number is the only thing that's useful, and it does help in a rush. But I'd cut it back, because most of what I eat is decided before I'm anywhere near a label. The photos get a bit more, and the text-only ones less.

{"points": {"A": 28, "B": 18, "C": 28, "D": 0, "E": 2, "F": 24}, "most_ignored": "D", "why": "Calorie badges are the only thing I'd actually use, but my snacks mostly come from a Sunday shop, so I've cut them back and given the photos more credit for sticking, even though I'd hate them."}

Fair enough, my friend has a point. I do say I'd resent things, and the cholesterol result did move me because it was frightening and came with a real number. A calorie badge on its own is easy to stop seeing by week two, especially on a Friday with the kids' biscuits in the trolley. The graphic photos would make me wince, and they would stick, so B was probably too low.

I still don't think the plain text does anything, and I'd tune out a label with no pictures and no numbers. The badge is still the only thing that gives me something to compare, and the photo alone gives me nothing to swap to. So the combination of fear and a figure is probably what would actually work on me, which makes F first. A and E drop a bit, and B goes up.

{"points": {"A": 16, "B": 18, "C": 0, "D": 2, "E": 24, "F": 40}, "most_ignored": "C", "why": "No label changes nothing, and plain text is just more wagging I'd tune out, whereas the photo plus a calorie number is the only combination that feels like my cholesterol scare."}

Fair point from my mate. I did say the photos would irritate me and I'd look away, so giving F 37 doesn't hang together. The badge is the only thing doing any work for me. The warnings are noise, and the pictures are worse than noise. I'll move most of F's points to B.

I'm still not convinced the badge would change much, though. A reduced Dairy Milk at 30p is a bargain whatever the number says, and on a long ride I want the calories. But the number next to the price does make me think per penny, and that's how I already work. So B gets the bulk, and A and F keep a few points for the small extra nudge.

{"points": {"A": 10, "B": 66, "C": 2, "D": 3, "E": 0, "F": 19}, "most_ignored": "E", "why": "The plain calorie badge is the only thing that fits how I already think about price and fuel, while warnings and gory photos are lectures I'd look past."}

Fair point on F versus E: if I say I'd roll my eyes at the text warning, it makes no sense to put F above E. The plain text adds nothing the calorie badge doesn't already give me, and it's the preachy bit. The photos at least do something, even if it's just a grim image in my head for a day.

I'm not convinced by the "feels like my sister talking" argument, though. She'd never shove a bowel tumour at me over a Dairy Milk, she'd just say eat decently and don't overthink it. Photos would put me off a bit, and I'd probably get annoyed too. They wouldn't change a Friday takeaway or a reduced chocolate bar. But I'll admit the calorie badge probably does less than I claimed, since I mostly buy on price and what's in the fridge. It would still be the one thing I'd actually use, mainly to spot the KIND bar isn't as light as I'd assumed. So C stays top, a bit lower, and E goes up.

{"points": {"A": 0, "B": 2, "C": 38, "D": 10, "E": 33, "F": 17}, "most_ignored": "A", "why": "Calorie numbers are still the only thing I'd use, but the photos would stick in my head more than I first said, while plain text warnings just feel preachy."}

Fair points, mostly. F at 25 doesn't hang together: I said plain warnings wash over me, so it's really just A with some wallpaper on it. It should sit a bit below A, not level with B.

The dentist point stings, because that's the one thing that actually worked on me. A person telling me off about something specific stuck, and a photo of a bowel tumour might do something similar in the moment. I'd still resent it and get used to it. But the badge on its own is the thing I'd use, because 95 versus 240 is easy to compare quickly, even if I'm already half-decided at the garage. So A stays on top, B gets a bit more because it has both the shock and the number, and E stays modest since there's no number to compare.

{"points": {"A": 35, "B": 30, "C": 0, "D": 2, "E": 15, "F": 18}, "most_ignored": "C", "why": "A clear calorie number is the fact I'd actually use to compare, the photos might bite briefly, and plain text warnings or no labels just fade into the background."}

Fair points, mostly. I did contradict myself: I said the photos would just irritate me and then handed them nearly half the points. F with no numbers and a big gory picture is the nagging I switch off from, so that drops right down. And yes, I'm not standing at a vending machine weighing 240 against 95 after the train. I'm on autopilot, so the numbers only help if they sink in over time and nudge the meal deal or the freezer choice.

I'm not convinced by the "professional talking straight" bit, though. The plain text warning is still the cancer line I've heard a hundred times, and my dentist worked because he was telling me off personally, not because of a label. So B stays small. D goes above E, because the calories do the useful work and the plain warning is less grating than a photo. A stays on top.

{"points": {"A": 42, "B": 6, "C": 2, "D": 24, "E": 22, "F": 4}, "most_ignored": "C", "why": "Calorie badges are the only thing giving me something usable, while the photos and cancer lines are nagging I'd tune out, and no labels at all does nothing."}

Fair point, and it stings a bit. I did stop the crisps because of boredom, not because a number told me to, and I do buy on price and yellow stickers, so a calorie badge is something I'd glance at and then ignore. I still think the photos are shouty and I'd resent them, but resenting something isn't the same as it not working. A bowel tumour on a Dairy Milk wrapper might make me put my hand back in the 3pm dead zone, where a tidy "240" wouldn't.

I'd still say the plain text warnings are mostly wallpaper, and I'd still give calories some credit, because when I do compare, the numbers help me pick the fruit pot over the shortbread. So I'm moving points toward the graphic options, especially C, which has the shock and the useful number. B drops, but not to the floor.

{"points": {"A": 22, "B": 20, "C": 30, "D": 14, "E": 0, "F": 4}, "most_ignored": "E", "why": "The graphic photos would probably jolt me out of bored autopilot more than a number or plain text would, though I'd still want the calorie badge for comparing when I'm actually choosing."}

Fair points, and a bit annoying because some of it lands. I do buy chocolate when I'm knackered, and by then I'm not comparing numbers, I'm just grabbing something. And the dentist thing did work because it was blunt and came from someone credible. So I'll shift some weight towards the graphic photos.

But I'd still say the numbers matter in the vending machine, because without them I can't tell the beetroot crisps from the McCoy's, and "healthy-looking" snacks are often not as light as they look. The photo with no numbers only tells me to avoid the treats, which is fine, but it's also the kind of thing I'd get numb to. Plain text warning is still wallpaper. Overall I'd give calories alone less, and the photo versions more, with E ahead because it has both.

{"points": {"A": 0, "B": 3, "C": 22, "D": 14, "E": 38, "F": 23}, "most_ignored": "A", "why": "Unlabelled snacks change nothing, and a bare text warning would fade into the background, whereas a graphic image plus numbers would actually bite on a tired Thursday."}

Fair points, partly. I do tend to say I'd use the numbers, and on a tired Friday I'd probably buy the Dairy Milk anyway. But the comparison at 1pm, 95 against 240, is a real nudge, and it works because it's concrete, like the booze thing was. The graphic photo would make me pause for a second, which is what my own numbers admitted, but it'd wear off fast and I'd resent it. Warnings without calories are still the telling-off I scroll past, so D and F stay low.

I'll move a bit away from the idea that I'm immune, shifting some points from C to E, since the photo plus numbers is where I'd actually hesitate. But C stays the biggest because it's the one I'd put up with every day.

{"points": {"A": 0, "B": 12, "C": 46, "D": 7, "E": 33, "F": 2}, "most_ignored": "A", "why": "Calorie badges give me a concrete comparison I'd actually use at the shelf, the graphic photo adds a brief pause on top, and warnings alone are just a telling-off I'd tune out."}
